~ start
Darwin: These statements look different… but they behave the same.
Logos: That is because truth can be rewritten without changing its meaning.

#De Morgan's Law
Logos: When negation is applied to a group, it changes both the operator and the statements.
Logos: The negation of P ∧ Q becomes ¬P ∨ ¬Q.
Logos: The negation of P ∨ Q becomes ¬P ∧ ¬Q.
Darwin: So I flip AND to OR… and negate both.
Logos: Yes.

#Double Negation
Logos: Two negations cancel each other. ¬(¬P) is simply P.

#Conditional Rewrite
Logos: A conditional can be rewritten. P → Q is equivalent to ¬P ∨ Q.”

#Conditional Variations (IMPORTANT TRAPS)
Logos: The converse, Q → P, is not equivalent.
Logos: The inverse, ¬P → ¬Q, is also not equivalent.
Logos: But the contrapositive, ¬Q → ¬P, is always equivalent.

Darwin: So only one transformation is actually safe.
Logos: Yes. The rest are common mistakes.

#Evaluation
Logos: Answer these before you proceed.
Logos: You must prove understanding.

